WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
When compiling a React native app on a local developer machine,

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.800
the build process often completes without issue,

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.000
but deploy that exact same code to a continuous integration server

4
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.500
and the pipeline fails entirely, blocked by an authentication error.

5
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:20.000
This discrepancy happens because Xcode defaults to automatic signing.

6
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.000
This setting hides the cryptographic complexity of iOS deployment,

7
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
resolving certificate conflicts behind the scenes on behalf of the developer.

8
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.000
Automatic signing relies on two hidden local dependencies to function,

9
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.000
the developer's populated macOS keychain and an interactive Apple ID session,

10
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.000
capable of receiving two-factor authentication prompts,

11
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.000
headless CI environments lack both.

12
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Ephemeral CI runners boot as blank slates without pre-existing keychains

13
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.000
and they cannot intercept a 2FA code sent to a developer's phone.

14
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.000
To automate iOS deployment, we have to strip away automatic signing.

15
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.000
We must entirely decouple cryptographic credentials

16
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.000
from individual developer's machines and interactive user sessions.

17
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.000
To rebuild this process for automation,

18
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.000
we have to look at the foundational component of iOS deployment,

19
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.000
the provisioning profile.

20
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
This mobile provision file acts as a signed envelope

21
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.000
tying together three strict requirements.

22
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.000
First, an app ID defining authorized capabilities.

23
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Second, a list of allowed device UUIDs,

24
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.000
the specific iPhones permitted to run the binary.

25
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.000
Third, the signing certificate containing the authorized public key.

26
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Apple issues two main types of certificates.

27
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.000
A development certificate is tied to a single user's Apple ID for local testing.

28
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.000
A distribution certificate is required for compiling release binaries

29
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.000
intended for test flight or the app store.

30
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.000
At launch, iOS reads this envelope.

31
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.000
It verifies that the certificate used to compile the binary perfectly matches

32
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.000
the public key embedded in this profile.

33
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.000
A valid build requires this exact triad.

34
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.000
If the machine's keys do not perfectly match the provisioning profile,

35
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.000
the entire compilation fails.

36
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.000
The private key for a distribution certificate is generated locally.

37
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.000
It lives exclusively in the local macOS keychain of the specific developer

38
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.000
who created it, not in the project repository.

39
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.000
If a new team member needs to build a release binary and lacks that private key,

40
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.000
Xcode's automatic signing will simply generate a brand new distribution certificate

41
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.000
for them.

42
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Apple limits the number of active distribution certificates per account.

43
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.000
To issue the new certificate, Apple automatically revokes the old one.

44
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.000
This creates a failure mode known as certificate roulette.

45
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.000
Revoking the previous certificate instantly invalidates the keychain

46
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.000
of every other developer and CI machine that relied on it.

47
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Their builds will now fail.

48
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.000
When distribution keys are siloed on individual laptops,

49
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.000
the entire team's ability to release is tied to a single machine's local state.

50
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:55.000
This ensures that every successful build for one developer effectively becomes a breaking change for the rest of the team.

51
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:59.000
Moving to a CI pipeline exposes this flaw immediately.

52
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.000
Ephemeral CI runners always boot in a completely blank state.

53
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.000
They possess no pre-populated keychains and carry no historical cryptographic data.

54
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.000
Because the CI runner is an automated script,

55
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.000
it absolutely cannot intercept a two-factor authentication prompt

56
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.000
to log into the Apple developer portal and dynamically generate missing keys.

57
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.000
The only viable solution is manual signing.

58
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:30.000
This requires that the precise distribution certificate is actively injected into a temporary keychain on the runner

59
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.000
before compilation begins.

60
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Simultaneously, the matching provisioning profile must be placed into a specific hard-coded path.

61
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.000
The mobile device slash provisioning profiles directory on the runner.

62
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.000
The runner authenticates with the repository using a personal access token,

63
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:53.000
retrieves the files, and uses the secret passphrase to decrypt and install the keys locally.

64
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:59.000
By utilizing a private secure Git repository as the strict authority for signing credentials,

65
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:03.000
we eliminate the certificate roulette problem entirely.

66
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:09.000
Furthermore, we also completely remove the need for any interactive Apple developer portal logins

67
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.000
during the continuous integration build process.

68
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:18.000
With the signing environment fully secured, we use Fastlane Jim to handle the actual compilation.

69
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.000
Jim executes a two-step process.

70
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:26.000
First, it compiles raw binary and metadata into an XC archive folder.

71
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.000
Second, it extracts that archive into a signed IPA file.

72
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.000
Next, Fastlane Pilot authenticates with the API and uploads that signed IPA payload directly to test flight.

73
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.000
Pilot accepts a skip waiting for build processing flag.

74
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.000
This ensures the command returns immediately upon upload,

75
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:51.000
preventing the CI runner from idling and burning execution minutes while Apple processes the binary on their back end.

76
00:04:51.000 --> 00:05:01.000
Fastlane deliver then handles the final presentation, synchronizing your version-controlled app metadata, release notes, and screenshots directly to the app store.

77
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:08.000
These tools abstract thousands of lines of fragile shell scripting into deterministic repeatable commands.

78
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.000
To orchestrate these tools, we use a fast file.

79
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:20.000
This is a declarative Ruby environment where match, Jim, Pilot, and deliver are sequenced into named execution blocks called lanes.

80
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.000
Lanes can execute other lanes.

81
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:30.000
This compositional structure allows you to build multi-target react native apps like compiling a main app and a separate notification extension.

82
00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:34.000
While keeping the logic modular and avoiding duplication.

83
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:44.000
The file barrier to this architecture is bypassing Apple ID authentication for actions that must communicate with AppStore Connect like Pilot and deliver.

84
00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.000
Apple provides an alternative to standard user logins.

85
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:52.000
The AppStore Connect API key, downloaded as a .p8 file.

86
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:59.000
By declaring this API key at the very start of a Fastlane lane, it creates a shared, headless authentication context.

87
00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:07.000
Every subsequent Fastlane action automatically utilizes this session, executing network calls to Apple without triggering a single 2FA prompt.

88
00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:11.000
Centralizing cryptography in a Git repository secures the build environment.

89
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:15.000
Authenticating via API keys removes the human bottleneck.

90
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:24.000
Together, they transition the iOS release process from an error-prone, localized chore into a scalable, fully automated function of your CI pipeline.

